#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097514} :activity/id #uuid "34909092-851c-5b8f-b289-94633fbc239b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097840 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to solve the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+z &=1 \\\\ y-z &=2 \\\\ z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix for this system can be written as \n\n$$\nM =\n\t\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}{\\color{red}1} & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & -1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{blue}0} & {\\color{red}1} & 3\\end{array}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix is in *row echelon form* (or REF). Whenever the augmented matrix is in row echelon form, we can solve the system using a method called **back substitution**.\n\nBack substitution involves\n\n- starting with the lowest equation (which is already solved for one variable),\n\n- substituting the result into the next-lowest equation to find the solution for another variable, and\n\n- continuing to substitute the result into the next-lowest equation until the values of all the variables have been found.\n\nHere, the third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny -(3) = 2 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = 5.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $y=5$ and $z=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx + 2(5) + (3) = 1\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx = -12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-12,$ $y=5,$ and $z=3.$ We can write it in vector form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\langle -12,5,3 \\right\\rangle = \n\t\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The values $x=-12,$ $y=5,$ and $z=3$ together form *one* solution. We say that this system has *one* solution, *not* three solutions. A system with three solutions would have three different solution vectors, whereas here, we only have *one* solution vector." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d84a9a0-94f9-57e2-a8e6-333ba26d72e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3149 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b94c8727-6dff-5c9a-9868-815a8a185978" :step/math-academy-id 17179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096456 :question/id #uuid "201a713a-83ca-5e31-9a4f-257795d7eb1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4690" :question/problem "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=3 \\\\ y+z &=2 \\\\ z &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The third equation states that $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(2)=2 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\ty = 2-2 = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $z=2$ and $y=0$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(0)+(2)=3\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx = 3-2 = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x = 1,$ $y=0,$ and $z = 2.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 1,0,2 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8f8937b-de15-5d4c-aa77-1d45595fc7af" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048826}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527304} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639199}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "47cad0ff-3b21-5238-b205-26619a15e0b5" :step/math-academy-id 17180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096457 :question/id #uuid "cc8ce97a-3bda-506f-b9d2-98ac7524583a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4691" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 5\\end{array} \n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $x_3=5.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(5)=-2\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_2=-2-5=-7.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 5$ and $x_2 = -7$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx_1+(-7)-(5) = 1\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_1 = 1 + 7 + 5 = 13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 13,$ $x_2=-7,$ and $x_3 = 5.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 13,-7,5 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de05090a-2461-514a-971d-b83c7cea124e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265734} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639248}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Using Back Substitution Given an Augmented Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "4f928835-23b6-530a-b253-305d43df52fa" :step/math-academy-id 17181 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097517}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097517 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096671 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096458 :question/id #uuid "7b44089c-8ca7-57e5-b390-7900647e3884" :question/math-academy-id "e-4692" :question/problem "Solve the system of equations that is represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 3\\end{array} \n\t\\right].\n$$\n\n" :question/worked-solution "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x_1,$ $x_2,$ and $x_3.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that\n\n$$\n3x_3=3 \n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \n\tx_3=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2+(1)=-2\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_2=-2-1=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute both $x_3 = 1$ and $x_2 = -3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x_1+(-3)-(1) = 0\n\t\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\tx_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2} (3 + 1) = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x_1 = 2,$ $x_2=-3,$ and $x_3 = 1.$ Or, in vector form, the solution is $\\langle 2,-3,1 \\rangle.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e51c9a-6bce-5ea6-a9fe-febb09f8754d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186639264}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations Given an Augmented Matrix Containing Non-Unit Leading Entries"} :step/id #uuid "a07d47d4-76d1-54a2-8790-392a617e65a8" :step/math-academy-id 17182}] :activity/title "Solving Systems of Equations Using Back Substitution" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096855}]}
